# Data flow diagrams (DFD): a recipe, to copy and adapt (pikslide --help
# dfd).
#
# A data flow diagram shows where data comes from, which processes
# transform it, where it's kept and where it goes. This one is a level-1
# DFD of order handling, in DeMarco/Yourdon notation, its parts drawn
# with the macros of dfd.pik.
#
# THE SYMBOLS, AND WHAT DRAWS EACH
# process: a numbered circle -- Take: dfdprocess("1", "Take" "order")
# data store: two parallel lines, its id and name between them --
#   Orders: dfdstore("D1", "Orders")
# external entity: a box -- Customer: dfdentity("Customer")
# data flow: an arrow, labeled with the data it carries -- arrow "order" above
# In Gane-Sarson notation, dfdgsprocess and dfdgsstore draw a process and
# a store instead, and the rest is the same.
#
# HOW IT'S LAID OUT
# - Label every flow with the data it carries, off the line: `above` a
#   level arrow, `ljust` (to the right) or `rjust` (to the left) beside an
#   upright one.
# - The top row chains left to right, each part after the arrow before it.
# - The second process hangs from the store it reads (with .n at 1in below
#   Orders.s), and the second store sits beside it (with .w at 1.2in right
#   of Ship.e), so that each flow between them is a straight line. A
#   store's .w and .e are its ends, its .n and .s the middle of its lines.
# - A flow that bends is a labeled `line` for its long segment, then an
#   `arrow` on from where it ends: a bent line's label would sit on none of
#   its segments (pikslide --help line).
# - A context diagram, level 0, is one process with only external entities
#   round it: the same parts, without data stores.

include "dfd.pik"

Customer: dfdentity("Customer")
arrow "order" above right 150%
Take: dfdprocess("1", "Take" "order")
arrow "new order" above right 150%
Orders: dfdstore("D1", "Orders")

Ship: dfdprocess("2", "Ship" "order") with .n at 1in below Orders.s
arrow "order details" ljust from Orders.s to Ship.n

Inventory: dfdstore("D2", "Inventory") with .w at 1.2in right of Ship.e
arrow "stock level" above from Inventory.w to Ship.e

line "delivery note" above from Ship.w left until even with Customer
arrow up to Customer.s
